    "narrative": "The offensive engine for the Austin Waves today is undoubtedly **Gunnar Henderson**, who leads the squad with an elite 82.40 score against Cade Cavalli. With the favorable matchup against a 4.06 xERA starter and the benefit of a leadoff spot, **Gunnar Henderson** is poised for high-leverage production. **Luis Garc\u00eda Jr.** follows closely at 74.56; despite facing the craftier Chris Bassitt, **Luis Garc\u00eda Jr.** benefits from a significant dynamic platoon advantage that elevates his floor. Similarly, **Heliot Ramos** (66.96) and **Kyle Tucker** (66.58) provide strong core value. **Heliot Ramos** is particularly well-positioned, as a favorable park factor combined with the opportunity to exploit Luis Severino\u2019s 4.63 xERA creates a prime environment for power. **Kyle Tucker** remains a must-start, as a combination of a favorable wind direction and his consistent volume profile overrides the sub-4.00 xERA of Jos\u00e9 Soriano.\n\nThe most difficult roster decision today involved the exclusion of **Nolan Schanuel**, who finished just outside the starting nine with a score of 50.80. While **Nolan Schanuel** was trending upward, his neutral platoon status against Justin Wrobleski couldn't quite displace the specialized utility of **Brady House** or the strategic slotting of **Sam Antonacci**, whose elite xwOBA (0.402) secured his spot in the lineup. We are closely monitoring the status of **Nasim Nunez** and **Brady House**; both face Chris Bassitt, and their efficiency is hampered by the fact that Bassitt is an elite holder of runners (57% SB). While **Nasim Nunez** is traditionally a green-light target for our stolen base strategy, the combination of his low spot in the order and the opposing battery\u2019s efficiency introduces risk to his projected output.\n\nAs we head into today\u2019s slate, managers should exercise caution regarding the \"Lineup Pending\" status across the board. The reliance on historical batting positions for **Liam Hicks**, **Drake Baldwin**, and **Miguel Vargas** introduces variance; should any of these players drop further down the order, their efficiency scores\u2014already hovering near our 50-point threshold\u2014will likely collapse. Furthermore, **Jackson Merrill** faces the toughest matchup of the day against the formidable Logan Gilbert (2.98 xERA). While his role as a leadoff hitter keeps him relevant, his low score of 52.65 makes him the primary candidate to monitor for a late-game swap if his efficiency wanes during early innings.",
